# Short referral links: pqp.gg/r/<code> -> the site with ?ref=<channel>
#
# WHY A PATH AND NOT A QUERY STRING. X strips the query off the URL in a
# profile's Website field (verified 22 Aug 2026: https://pqp.gg/?ref=x-bio
# saved fine and resolved to a bare https://pqp.gg/), so a bio link cannot
# carry attribution as a parameter. It can carry a path. The same short form
# is worth having anywhere the raw URL is read by a human, a Reddit comment
# above all, where a full utm_source/utm_medium/utm_campaign triple reads as
# marketing and ?ref= does not.
#
# These must stay above the SPA catch-all: Pages takes the first match.
# `ref` is picked up by client/src/lib/acquisition.ts like any UTM parameter.
#
# Deliberately an explicit list and not `/r/* -> /?ref=:splat`: a wildcard
# would let any crawler that guesses a path invent a channel name and put it
# in the report as if it were one of ours.
/r/x          /?ref=x-bio          302
/r/reddit     /?ref=reddit         302
/r/github     /?ref=github         302
/r/yt         /?ref=youtube        302
/r/ig         /?ref=instagram      302
/r/tt         /?ref=tiktok         302
# The Android closed beta. Points at the page, not at Play: a closed test
# admits nobody who has not joined the tester group first, so a link straight
# to the opt-in URL sends people to a Google page that does nothing for them.
/r/android    /download?ref=android-beta   302

